<resources xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
           xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://developer.garmin.com/downloads/connect-iq/resources.xsd">
  <!--
    Icon set. GENERATED by tools/gen_icons.py — edit that, not the SVGs.

    scaleRelativeTo="screen" makes the resource compiler rasterize per device, so
    one asset set serves both 416 and 454 with no per-device qualifier folders.
    Hero icons are 13% of screen width (54px @416, 59px @454) and chip icons 6.7%
    (28px / 30px). Those percentages must stay in sync with Grid.iconHero and
    Grid.iconChip — BitmapReference does not expose its own dimensions, so the
    draw size has to be known in code rather than read back from the resource.

    automaticPalette="false" is MANDATORY. It defaults to TRUE on 16-bit-colour
    devices, which reduces each image to a at most 256-colour palette with a single
    transparent index — that destroys the anti-aliased edges runtime tinting
    depends on, and makes the asset unusable as a BufferedBitmap source
    (setAntiAlias throws InvalidPaletteException on a paletted buffer).

    scope="application" keeps all 46 out of the GLANCE build. Without it the
    glance links the whole resource table and dies with "Out Of Memory Error" —
    it gets 64KB, a twelfth of the watch app, and was measured at 57.4/59.8KB.

    packingFormat="default" is "fastest to load" and supports alpha. png packs
    smaller on disk but is "slowest to load, which can add runtime cost if purged
    and reloaded frequently from the graphics pool" — exactly this access
    pattern. At ~5.8KB each these cost ~270KB of a 64MB filespace.
  -->
  <bitmap id="IcSquat" filename="icons/pattern/squat.svg"
          scaleX="13%" scaleY="13%" scaleRelativeTo="screen"
          automaticPalette="false" packingFormat="default"
          scope="application" />
